What is Jenkins?
Jenkins, one of the most widely used Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery (CI/CD) automation tools.

What is Jenkins?
Jenkins is an open-source automation server written in Java.
It helps developers build, test, and deploy code automatically, reducing manual work.
It integrates with tools like Git, Maven, Docker, Kubernetes, Ansible, AWS, and many more.

Why Jenkins?
Supports automation of repetitive tasks in software development.
Ensures faster delivery of applications through continuous integration.
Provides hundreds of plugins for integration with popular tools.
Enables teams to detect issues early by running automated tests.

Key Features
Pipeline as Code → Define CI/CD pipelines in a Jenkinsfile.
Plugin Ecosystem → Extend functionality with 1800+ plugins.
Cross-platform → Works on Linux, Windows, macOS.
Distributed Builds → Run builds on multiple machines for scalability.
Web-based Dashboard → Easy monitoring of jobs & pipelines.

Basic Workflow
Developer pushes code to GitHub/GitLab.
Jenkins automatically triggers a build (via webhook).
Code is compiled, tested, packaged, and deployed.
Notifications are sent to the team (Slack/Email).

Real-World Use
Automating software builds.
Running unit and integration tests.
Deploying applications to servers or Kubernetes clusters.
Monitoring pipelines and ensuring delivery speed.

Summary:
Jenkins is the backbone of modern DevOps workflows, enabling teams to automate CI/CD pipelines and deliver software faster, more reliably, and with less manual intervention.
